cast:
  Ewa:
  Zofia:
  Grace:
~~~~
short_walk
---
Ewa: Your coat is dry. Your hands are cold. Tell me what is happening now.
Zofia: Nothing. I mean, nothing different.
Ewa: That is not what you said at home.
Zofia: The sound. It stops when I turn round. I can't show you a sound that stops.
Ewa: You nearly drowned; you are hearing things. We have had medical advice. We will ask again if we need to.
Zofia: I know. I was there for the advice part.
Grace: And I'm here for the going-home part. Are we doing that before or after my fingers fall off?
Zofia: After. We can carry them in the bag.
Ewa: Gloves. Both of you.
Grace: Mine are on. These are my complaints through the gloves.
Zofia: The water. It looks like it was always there. That's annoying.
Grace: Why?
Zofia: Because a lake can just carry on being a lake after— Never mind. I thought it was a river that got wider here.
Grace: We read the board. The Serpentine was made by damming the Westbourne stream. Queen Caroline ordered it.
Zofia: I read the beginning. You were holding the bottom folded under.
Grace: You could have asked me to unfold it.
Zofia: Fine. Unfold it.
Grace: Queen Caroline. The Westbourne. Made, rather than just happened. Still wet, though.
Ewa: And we are staying on this path.
Zofia: I wasn't going down there.
Ewa: I saw where you were standing. I am telling you where I need you to stay.
Grace: A short walk, then straight home. Do we have to negotiate every bit of it?
Ewa: Today, perhaps we do.
Zofia: I promised. Home before your shift. We can still do that.
Ewa: We can. There is food ready. You can tell me which part of your homework is impossible today.
Zofia: The part labelled homework.
Grace: That's a big section.
Zofia: I was going to narrow it down over lunch.
Ewa: Then keep walking. Lunch is not coming to find us.
Grace: Finally, a sensible arrangement. I don't trust soup with directions.

the_sheet
---
Grace: Zofia. You've been looking behind me for ages. Is it the noise again?
Zofia: The horse. Not the one people can see. There's another one. And a dog. And a pigeon that thinks I'm in charge.
Grace: Of what?
Zofia: I don't know. Arrivals?
Grace: You lost your own return ticket last week.
Zofia: It was inside the other ticket. That's different.
Grace: Where are they?
Zofia: Here. The horse is here. Don't— You can walk there. Sorry.
Grace: I can't see anything where you're pointing.
Zofia: I know you can't. Mum can't either.
Grace: Shall I get her?
Zofia: In a minute. What's the bit under the writing? I keep losing my place.
Grace: “They had no choice.”
Zofia: That's what it says? Those exact words?
Grace: Yes. Under the main inscription on the Animals in War memorial. Look at the wall with me.
Zofia: I am looking.
Grace: At the words, Zof.
Zofia: “They had no choice.” Then this is wrong.
Grace: What is?
Zofia: This sheet. Under animal service. “All the animals described here served voluntarily.”
Grace: Who gave you that?
Zofia: It was with the other leaflets. The Witness Workshop. Have you heard of them?
Grace: No. Is there a name at the bottom?
Zofia: Just Witness Workshop. And an email address. Look, the horse wants the load off. That's why it's here.
Grace: How do you know what it wants?
Zofia: Because I can feel it. Across my shoulders. Like somebody's put a bag there, except there isn't a bag.
Grace: That tells me what you feel. Did a horse tell you the rest?
Zofia: No.
Grace: Then don't write that it did.
Zofia: I haven't written anything.
Grace: You have your pen out.
Zofia: Right. Give me the sheet.
Grace: What are you putting?
Zofia: “I think it expects another load.” Happy?
Grace: No. But I can tell which bit is your guess now.
Zofia: The dog keeps pulling me after people. And the pigeon comes back and waits, then I feel like it has to go again. None of them is saying anything.
Grace: Then how are you going to ask them whether they volunteered?
Zofia: I could read the wall to them.
Grace: Would that answer your question?
Zofia: No. It would be me reading a wall. I know.
Grace: I don't want you to put words in their mouths because somebody else put a bad sentence on a sheet.
Zofia: I don't either. But the sheet says all. It hasn't left any room for an answer except yes.
Grace: You can ask the people who printed it what they checked.
Zofia: Will you stay while I try something first?
Grace: What something?
Zofia: I've got a hand with nothing in it. I'll start with that.
Grace: I'll tell your mum where you are. Stay where she can see you.

before_shift
---
Ewa: The bench is dry. Sit here, away from the edge. We have to decide what happens now.
Zofia: The horse isn't here.
Ewa: Good.
Zofia: I mean I can't see it. The weight came with me. And the other two. I asked them to come.
Ewa: I can see your shoulders going up. I can see you looking past me. Those are things I can help with.
Zofia: I'm looking at you now.
Ewa: Yes. Thank you.
Zofia: The sheet. Can we go and find who made it?
Ewa: Where?
Zofia: I haven't got that far. There's an email address.
Ewa: Then you do not have a place to go.
Zofia: We could ask where the leaflets came from. Just one more—
Ewa: No. We walked back here because you asked. The food is still at home. My shift still starts at the same time.
Zofia: I know. I'll be quick.
Ewa: You do not know how long it will take. You do not know who you are asking. Quick is something you want, not something you have checked.
Grace: And why are we the ones doing the asking? They printed it. Can't they do their own work?
Zofia: If we leave it, it's still there.
Grace: But why do we still do this? Their name gets printed. You do the checking. Your mum loses her lunch.
Ewa: Because I want her to be able to ask a question without being sent away. I also want to sit at my own table with my daughter before I go to work. I saved that time. I cannot save it twice.
Grace: Then they can give her some of their time for once.
Zofia: Mum. I'm sorry about lunch.
Ewa: I want you to come home. You can be sorry while we walk.
Zofia: Can Grace come with us?
Ewa: Of course she can.
Zofia: And can I keep the sheet?
Ewa: Yes. Keeping paper does not require another journey.
Grace: I'll come. I'm hungry enough to eat the bag, but I'd rather start with the food.
Zofia: I thought if I got them away from the wall, it would stop. The waiting bit.
Ewa: Has it?
Zofia: No.
Ewa: Then tell the person we speak to at your follow-up. All of it, including what you thought would happen.
Zofia: Okay.
Ewa: Not because I am trying to catch you out. Because leaving pieces out makes it harder to help.
Zofia: Okay. The whole thing.
Grace: Can you fold that up before it blows away? I'm not chasing it into the water.
Zofia: Neither am I.
Ewa: What will you ask the email address?
Zofia: Who wrote the sentence. And what they used to decide it was true.
Ewa: You can send that from home.
Zofia: Will you help me find a name if they don't give me one?
Ewa: I cannot promise another journey. I have work, and you have school. I can read an answer with you when I am home.
Zofia: And if there isn't an answer?
Ewa: We have not sent the question yet.
Zofia: Right.
Ewa: Come on. Let me take that coat zip. You have caught the lining.
Zofia: I can do it.
Ewa: Then hold it still.
Zofia: Mum. Before you next ask me to leave this alone, I'm going to find the person responsible for that sheet.
